The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
INIT_DELAYED_WORK(&epf_group->cfs_work, pci_epf_cfs_work);
queue_delayed_work(system_wq, &epf_group->cfs_work, msecs_to_jiffies(1));
configfs_register_group(&epf_group->group, secondary_epc_group);
```
Reason:
The asynchronous creation of sub-groups via a delayed work queue introduces a race condition. The `INIT_DELAYED_WORK` and `queue_delayed_work` schedule `pci_epf_cfs_work` to run later. By the time the work executes, the parent configfs directory (`epf_group->group`) may have been removed (e.g., by `rmdir`). When `configfs_register_group` tries to access the parent group's structures, the parent is no longer valid, leading to a NULL pointer dereference.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
configfs_add_default_group(secondary_epc_group, &epf_group->group);
configfs_add_default_group(primary_epc_group, &epf_group->group);
configfs_add_default_group(group, &epf_group->group);
pci_epf_cfs_add_sub_groups(epf_group);
```
Reason:
The fix replaces the asynchronous `configfs_register_group` calls with synchronous `configfs_add_default_group` calls. The new function `pci_epf_cfs_add_sub_groups` is invoked directly from `pci_epf_make` before returning the group. This ensures that all sub-groups are created immediately and atomically, preventing any use-after-free or null-pointer dereference because the parent group still exists when the sub-groups are added. The delayed work infrastructure is completely removed.

